title: OVHcloud CLI Release Swap
page: breaches/ovhcloud-cli-release-swap.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: Per OVHcloud, a developer's compromised GitHub account was used on October 19 to replace the binaries on five ovhcloud-cli releases with a stealer build; a customer's pinned hash in CI caught it.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 1h33 | swapped binaries available, per OVHcloud | 12:47 to 14:21 UTC, October 19
stat: 5 × 5 | releases × platform binaries replaced | v0.11.0 through v0.15.0, per OVHcloud
stat: ~250 | downloads in the window, per OVHcloud | a download count, not machines that ran it
OpenSourceMalware's headline names GlassWorm; its report calls the March-to-October link an inference. OVHcloud ties the extension's wallet to GlassWorm, without naming the October uploader.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

The swapped binaries were available from 12:47 to 14:21 UTC on October 19, per OVHcloud, which gives the duration as 1h33.

Five releases, v0.11.0 through v0.15.0, each had five platform binaries replaced, per OVHcloud.

About 250 downloads came during that window, from OVHcloud's download statistics. That is a download count. None of the sources the page reviewed gives a number of machines that ran the binary, or reports stolen credentials being used.

The threat actor is the hedged part. OpenSourceMalware's headline names GlassWorm, on the strength of a wallet on public GlassWorm indicator lists and a matching force-push technique in the account's March activity, though its report calls the March-to-October link an inference. OVHcloud ties the 2025 extension's wallet to GlassWorm and traces the account's compromise to that extension, without naming who made the October uploads.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: October 20, 2025 | The developer installs color-picker-universal on a personal MacBook
event: March 5, 2026 | The attacker's earliest server-verified use of the account
event: October 13 | Commit 5ead8ef reaches main; the attacker later builds from it
event: October 19, 12:47 UTC | The v0.15.0 Darwin binary is replaced first
event: 13:50 UTC | A customer whose pinned hash failed in CI opens issue #300
event: 14:21 UTC | OVHcloud removes the binaries; its advisory follows October 20
OVHcloud traces the account's compromise to the extension. March 5 is OpenSourceMalware's date, and it calls the link between March and October its own inference. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this timeline is one the page gives. The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

On October 20, 2025, the developer whose GitHub account was later used installed the color-picker-universal extension, version 2.8.91, on a personal MacBook. OVHcloud traces the account's compromise to that extension.

March 5, 2026 is the earliest server-verified use of the account by the attacker, per OpenSourceMalware, about 228 days before the swap. OpenSourceMalware calls the link between that March misuse and the October uploads its own inference.

Commit 5ead8ef was merged to main on October 13 and was never a release tag. The stealer build was compiled from it, with uncommitted changes.

On October 19 the v0.15.0 Darwin binary was replaced at 12:47 UTC. A customer whose CI pinned one archive's SHA-256 saw the job fail and opened issue #300 at 13:50. OVHcloud began investigating at 13:57, removed the malicious binaries at 14:21, 31 minutes after the report, and published its advisory on October 20.

== Who reported what
layout: table
row: Source | What the page takes from it
row: OVHcloud's advisory | Timeline, replaced files, stealer targets, the extension
row: The customer | Issue #300; a releases API capture of v0.13.0 to v0.15.0
row: OpenSourceMalware | Account timeline from server-side records; GlassWorm
row: StepSecurity | Replay on its own rebuild; install paths and hashes
row: The Hacker News | Upgrade path limits; Homebrew tap history; GlassWorm
OVHcloud's advisory rests on the actual binaries. The Hacker News wrote before it appeared; StepSecurity's replay used a rebuild.
notes:
The page draws on several sources and says which one stands behind each claim.

OVHcloud published GitHub security advisory GHSA-862f-55f5-vmvw on October 20, 2026. It gives the timeline, the files replaced, what the stealer gathers, and the 2025 extension. It rests on the actual binaries: it gives their MD5 hashes, lists five samples on VirusTotal and thanks the VirLabs.ai malware analysis platform.

The customer opened issue #300 and posted the go version -m output there. The customer's capture from the GitHub releases API covers v0.15.0, v0.14.0 and v0.13.0, with their checksums files.

OpenSourceMalware built the account's timeline from GitHub's server-side records rather than commit dates, and its headline names GlassWorm.

StepSecurity replayed the stealer on its own rebuild, because the real binaries were deleted, and set out which install paths checked a hash.

The Hacker News wrote before the advisory appeared. It limits which installs the upgrade command exposed and checked the Homebrew tap's history. Its March 2026 reporting on GlassWorm is the reporting OVHcloud cites for the extension's wallet.

== What the GlassWorm name rests on
layout: compare
col: The GlassWorm name rests on | A wallet on public GlassWorm indicator lists, in March loaders, per OpenSourceMalware | The same wallet in the 2025 extension; OVHcloud says it belongs to GlassWorm | An amend-and-force-push method that OpenSourceMalware and StepSecurity match to ForceMemo
col: What the sources leave open | OpenSourceMalware calls the March-to-October link its own inference | The stealer used a different wallet | OVHcloud does not name the actor behind the October uploads
When The Hacker News wrote, no swapped archive had been analyzed in public, so nothing from the October binary had been tied to GlassWorm.
notes:
The page puts two caveats next to its headline. This slide is the first: what the GlassWorm name rests on.

It rests on a Solana wallet and a force-push technique. OpenSourceMalware found the wallet, which appears on public GlassWorm indicator lists, in loaders force-pushed to the account's personal repositories in March 2026. It matched the amend-and-force-push method to the ForceMemo campaign, as StepSecurity also does. OVHcloud found the same wallet in the extension and says it belongs to GlassWorm.

Three things stay open. OpenSourceMalware calls the link between the March misuse and the October uploads its own inference. The stealer used a different wallet from the one OVHcloud ties to GlassWorm. And OVHcloud does not name the actor behind the October uploads.

When The Hacker News wrote, no copy of a swapped archive had been analyzed in public, so nothing from the October binary had been tied to GlassWorm.

The second caveat is on the At a glance slide: about 250 is a download count, not a count of machines that ran the binary.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | IDE extension | T1176.002
step: 02 | Persistence | Account misused | T1078.004
step: 03 | Initial Access | Local build | T1195.002
step: 04 | Initial Access | Releases swapped | T1036.005
step: 05 | Execution | No hash checks | T1204.002
step: 06 | Credential Access | Files copied | T1552.001
step: 07 | Exfiltration | Solana, upload | T1041
Arrows show the page's stage order. OVHcloud traces the account's compromise to the stage 01 extension; OpenSourceMalware calls the March-to-October link an inference.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase label and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01, Initial Access, is the color-picker-universal extension on the developer's personal MacBook in October 2025. Stage 02, Persistence, is the developer's GitHub account in the attacker's use by March 2026. Stage 03, Initial Access, is the local build from an October 13 commit with an envchecker module added. Stage 04, Initial Access, is the October 19 upload over five existing releases, with the checksums file replaced on at least three of them. Stage 05, Execution, covers the install paths that checked no hash. Stage 06, Credential Access, is the stealer copying credential files. Stage 07, Exfiltration, is the Solana RPC calls and the upload.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order, with two hedges. OVHcloud traces the account's compromise to the extension, but says the October uploads used stolen keys rather than the personal MacBook, without saying how the keys were taken. And OpenSourceMalware calls the link between the March misuse and the October uploads its own inference.

== OVHcloud traces the compromise to an extension on a personal MacBook
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1176.002 | Software Extensions: IDE Extensions
fact: Installed | color-picker-universal 2.8.91, October 20, 2025
fact: Persistence | a NodeJS service registered through a plist
fact: Contacted | 162.33.177[.]177
fact: Wallet | OVHcloud says it is known to belong to GlassWorm
OVHcloud does not say what the extension took from the laptop.
notes:
Stage 01 starts a year before the swap. On October 20, 2025, the developer whose GitHub account was later used installed the extension color-picker-universal, version 2.8.91, on a personal MacBook. The page maps this to T1176.002, IDE Extensions. IDE stands for integrated development environment, such as VS Code.

A persistent NodeJS service was registered through a plist, a macOS property list file, and communicated with 162.33.177[.]177. The extension makes RPC calls, meaning remote procedure calls, with a Solana wallet that OVHcloud says is known to belong to the GlassWorm threat actor, citing The Hacker News's March 2026 reporting. That reporting describes GlassWorm as a campaign that spreads through malicious VS Code and Cursor extensions and has moved on to taking over GitHub accounts with stolen tokens.

OVHcloud does not say what the extension took from the laptop. It says the developer's OVHcloud laptop had not been compromised, as far as it knew when it wrote the advisory.

The next slide explains IDE extensions in general.

== What ATT&CK calls IDE extensions
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1176.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1176/002/
IDEs such as Visual Studio Code support extensions that add features. ATT&CK says a malicious one can come through a marketplace or be side-loaded, and once installed it runs every time the IDE launches.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes adversaries abusing an integrated development environment extension to establish persistent access to victim systems. IDEs such as Visual Studio Code, IntelliJ IDEA and Eclipse support extensions: software components that add features like code linting, auto-completion, task automation, or integration with tools like Git and Docker.

A malicious extension can be installed through an extension marketplace or side-loaded directly into the IDE. Adversaries may also leverage benign extensions; ATT&CK's example is persistent SSH tunnels through the VS Code Remote SSH extension.

Trust is typically established through the installation process. Once installed, a malicious extension runs every time the IDE is launched, and ATT&CK says it can then execute arbitrary code, establish a backdoor, mine cryptocurrency or exfiltrate data.

== By March 2026 the attacker was using the developer's GitHub account
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Persistence
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: First verified use | March 5, 2026, force-pushing a loader, per OpenSourceMalware
fact: Account | personal, in OVHcloud's organization, which requires 2FA
fact: October uploads | "stolen keys", per OVHcloud; type and theft not disclosed
fact: Same actor? | the March-to-October link is OpenSourceMalware's inference
With no commits in the repository, the account could still upload release assets.
notes:
OpenSourceMalware built its timeline from GitHub's server-side records rather than commit dates, which the pusher controls. On March 5 and 6, 2026, four of the account's personal repositories were force-pushed with a hidden preinstall.js loader, or the same decoder inlined in a source file. The loader reads a link from the memo of a Solana transaction, using the same wallet as the extension. The page maps this stage to T1078.004, Cloud Accounts.

OpenSourceMalware calls March 5 the earliest server-verified use of the account by the attacker, about 228 days before the swap, and treats it as an upper bound on when the attacker had a token. OVHcloud's advisory also records the March compromise and the reused wallet.

The account was the developer's personal GitHub account, registered to OVHcloud's organization, which requires two-factor authentication, 2FA, on every account. It was not a public member and had no commits in ovh/ovhcloud-cli, yet held write access; OpenSourceMalware infers that access most likely came through the repository's owners team.

OVHcloud says the October uploads used stolen keys rather than the personal MacBook, and does not say what kind of keys or how they were taken. OpenSourceMalware names an infostealer on the developer's machine or a stolen token as the likeliest paths.

== The attacker compiled the CLI locally from an October 13 commit
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
fact: Built from | 5ead8ef, merged to main October 13, never a release tag
fact: Version string | v0.15.1-0.20261013083505-5ead8ef9b43d+dirty
fact: Added | an envchecker stealer module, stripped
fact: Untouched | source code and build chain, per OVHcloud
Missing the release pipeline's version flag, it did not come from that pipeline.
notes:
The go version -m output that the customer posted in issue #300, and that OVHcloud reproduces, shows a pseudo-version built from commit 5ead8ef with uncommitted changes. A pseudo-version is a version string Go derives from a commit that carries no release tag. That commit was merged to main on October 13 and was never a release tag. The page maps this stage to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain.

The build used the linker flags -s and -w and lacked the version flag the project's GoReleaser configuration sets, so it did not come from the release pipeline. OVHcloud describes the binary as the original CLI recompiled with stripped malicious Go code, an envchecker module, and says the source code and build chain were not affected.

The +dirty suffix is a weak marker: The Hacker News found it on the clean v0.16.0 binary too. StepSecurity names the pseudo-version and the missing version flag as the reliable ones.

Two analyses read an earlier event differently. On October 15 the account deleted two branches in ovh/ovhcloud-docs. StepSecurity says the names read like CI-trigger probing. OpenSourceMalware notes the account created both in May and September, reads the deletion as cleanup, and says GitHub's data cannot show whether the owner or the attacker made it.

== What ATT&CK calls compromising the software supply chain
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1195.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/002/
ATT&CK describes manipulating software before a final consumer receives it. Ways include changing the source code, changing the update or distribution mechanism, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain. Adversaries may manipulate application software before it reaches a final consumer, for the purpose of data or system compromise.

It names several ways this can happen: manipulating the application's source code, manipulating the update or distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.

ATT&CK adds that targeting may be specific to a chosen set of victims. Or the software may be distributed to a broad set of consumers, with the adversary moving on to further tactics only against specific victims.

== On October 19 the account uploaded stealer builds over five existing releases
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1036.005 | Masquerading: Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location
fact: First replacement | 12:47 UTC, the v0.15.0 Darwin binary
fact: Replaced | Darwin arm64 and x86_64, Linux arm64 and x86_64, Windows x86_64
fact: Checksums file | replaced on three releases, per the customer; inferred on two
fact: Caught | a pinned SHA-256 in a customer's CI; issue #300 at 13:50 UTC
A trusted version began delivering different code, at unchanged URLs.
notes:
OVHcloud's timeline, converted to UTC: the v0.15.0 Darwin binary was replaced at 12:47, all five targeted v0.15.0 binaries by 12:57, and the binaries on v0.14.0 back to v0.11.0 by 13:30. The page maps this stage to T1036.005, Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location: the same file names, on the same releases.

The replaced files were the Darwin arm64 and x86_64, Linux arm64 and x86_64, and Windows x86_64 builds. The i386, Windows arm64 and WebAssembly builds were left alone.

On at least three of the releases the checksums file was replaced too, after the binaries it checked, so the published hashes matched the new files. OVHcloud's advisory does not mention the checksums file. The next slide sets out how the records differ on when the replacements ended.

OVHcloud calls the main issue a repository not configured for release immutability, so an account with write access could replace assets on releases published weeks or months earlier, at unchanged URLs.

A customer whose CI pinned the SHA-256 of the v0.15.0 Linux arm64 archive saw the job fail and opened issue #300 at 13:50 UTC. OVHcloud began investigating at 13:57, removed all the malicious binaries at 14:21, and found no changes by the account in its other repositories.

== When the last replacement came, by record
layout: table
row: Record | Releases it covers | Replacements
row: The customer's releases API capture | v0.13.0 to v0.15.0 | about 12:47 to 13:12 UTC; last checksums file at 13:11
row: StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware | all five, extending the customer's capture | ending about 13:12 UTC
row: OVHcloud's timeline | all five | all replaced by 13:30 UTC
OpenSourceMalware says it could not confirm that extension and end time independently. OVHcloud's advisory does not mention the checksums file.
notes:
Three records disagree on when the replacements ended, and the page sets them side by side without choosing.

The customer's capture from the GitHub releases API covers only v0.15.0, v0.14.0 and v0.13.0, replaced between about 12:47 and 13:12 UTC. It shows the checksums file on each of them replaced after the binaries it checked, the last at 13:11.

StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware extend that capture to all five releases and put the end at about 13:12, which OpenSourceMalware says it could not confirm independently. OpenSourceMalware infers the same set of files for v0.11.0 and v0.12.0.

OVHcloud's timeline has all five replaced by 13:30, and its advisory does not mention the checksums file.

So the checksums replacement is recorded for three releases and inferred for the other two. When you tell this part, say at least three.

== The install script and the CLI's upgrade command checked no hash
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Execution
technique: T1204.002 | User Execution: Malicious File
fact: install.sh | no hash check, per StepSecurity and The Hacker News
fact: ovhcloud upgrade | verifies nothing, per StepSecurity
fact: Also exposed | direct downloads, and CI jobs fetching without a pinned hash
fact: Downloads | about 250, per OVHcloud; no source counts machines that ran it
Homebrew's pinned cask would have refused the swapped file, per StepSecurity.
notes:
StepSecurity and The Hacker News agree that install.sh never checks a hash, and StepSecurity says the CLI's ovhcloud upgrade command verifies nothing either. The page maps this stage to T1204.002, User Execution: Malicious File.

The Hacker News limits the upgrade path to v0.12.0, v0.13.0 and v0.14.0 installs on macOS or Linux that were set up by the script or by hand; on Homebrew and go install setups the command only prints what to run. Direct downloads and CI jobs that fetched the archives without a pinned hash were exposed for the same reason. OpenSourceMalware, StepSecurity and The Hacker News all say that go install, source builds and the Docker image were not exposed.

OVHcloud counts about 250 downloads in the window. That is a download figure, and no source gives a count of machines that ran the binary.

StepSecurity says Homebrew's cask pins per-architecture SHA-256 hashes, so Homebrew would have refused the swapped file. The sources disagree on two paths, Homebrew among them, and the next slide sets them out.

== Install paths, and where the sources disagree
layout: table
row: Path | What the sources say
row: install.sh | No hash check, per StepSecurity and The Hacker News
row: install.sh, arm64 Linux | Never worked, per StepSecurity
row: ovhcloud upgrade | Verifies nothing, per StepSecurity
row: Upgrade exposure | v0.12.0 to v0.14.0 script or manual installs, macOS or Linux, per The Hacker News
row: Homebrew | Exposed, per OpenSourceMalware; the pinned cask would have refused it, per StepSecurity
All three say go install, source builds and the Docker image were not exposed. OpenSourceMalware and The Hacker News make no arm64 distinction.
notes:
The sources agree on most install paths and disagree on two.

Homebrew is the first. OpenSourceMalware listed a Homebrew install or upgrade as exposed. The Hacker News checked the tap's history and found no change between the September cask and the v0.16.0 cask, and StepSecurity says the cask pins per-architecture SHA-256 hashes, so Homebrew would have refused the swapped file.

arm64 Linux is the second. StepSecurity reports that install.sh never worked on arm64 Linux, because it requests an asset name that was never published. That would leave arm64 Linux exposed only through the upgrade command or a manual download. The exposure lists from OpenSourceMalware and The Hacker News do not make that distinction.

For the upgrade command, The Hacker News limits exposure to v0.12.0, v0.13.0 and v0.14.0 installs on macOS or Linux that were set up by the script or by hand. On Homebrew and go install setups, the command only prints what to run.

OpenSourceMalware, StepSecurity and The Hacker News all say go install, source builds and the Docker image were not exposed.

== Each run copied credential files into a new envchecker folder
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
fact: Cloud | AWS, Google Cloud, Azure and DigitalOcean credentials
fact: Infrastructure | Kubernetes, Terraform and Docker credentials
fact: Developer | SSH keys, .netrc, global Git configuration, npm configuration
fact: AI coding tool data | VS Code, Cursor, Continue, Cody, OpenCode, Hermes, Claude
OVHcloud says the folder is never deleted, so it is a way to check for exposure.
notes:
OVHcloud's advisory lists what the stealer gathers: AWS credentials and configuration including S3, Google Cloud, Azure, DigitalOcean's doctl, Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform credentials, npm configuration, SSH keys, .netrc and the global Git configuration. The page maps this stage to T1552.001, Credentials In Files.

It also takes local data from AI coding tools: the VS Code and Cursor state databases, the Continue extension's index, Sourcegraph Cody chat histories, the OpenCode database, Hermes agent sessions and the Claude agent's configuration directory. Besides copying files, it writes out credentials collected through various commands, which the advisory does not list.

On each run the binary creates a new folder in the temporary directory, named envchecker- followed by nine or ten digits, copies the files into it and archives it. OVHcloud says the folder is never deleted, which makes it a way to check for exposure.

The advisory's path list does not include ~/.ovh.conf, where The Hacker News notes the CLI keeps OVH API credentials, but OVHcloud's advice covers every secret stored on an affected machine.

The next slide explains credentials in files in general.

== What ATT&CK calls credentials in files
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1552.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/001/
ATT&CK says adversaries may search local file systems for insecurely stored credentials. In cloud and container environments, user and service account credentials often sit in local configuration and credential files, which can be copied and reused elsewhere.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK describes adversaries searching local file systems and remote file shares for files that contain insecurely stored credentials. These can be files users create to store their own credentials, shared credential stores, configuration files containing passwords for a system or service, or source code and binaries with embedded passwords.

In cloud and containerized environments, it says, authenticated user and service account credentials are often stored in local configuration and credential files. They may also turn up as parameters to deployment commands in container logs.

In some cases these files can be copied and reused on another machine, or their contents read and then used to authenticate without copying any files.

== Solana RPC calls, then the upload
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1041 | Exfiltration Over C2 Channel
fact: Solana RPC | one of five public endpoints, chosen at random
fact: Stealer's wallet | BHaDaVbdCYFNaKZGn7yLXFneWoJHZgLiBYkHxVrjyALF
fact: Upload | hxxps://45.32.148[.]174/upload, which OVHcloud says can change
fact: Other wallet | BjVe..., which OVHcloud ties to GlassWorm
When The Hacker News wrote, no swapped archive had been analyzed in public, so nothing from the October binary had been tied to GlassWorm.
notes:
OVHcloud calls the mechanism rudimentary, with one more elaborate part. The stealer picks one of five public Solana RPC endpoints at random and makes its calls with the wallet that begins BHaDa, then uploads the archive to 45.32.148[.]174. The address is written defanged, with hxxps and [.], so it cannot be clicked. The page maps this stage to T1041, Exfiltration Over C2 Channel; C2 means command and control. OVHcloud warns that the threat actor can change this URL at any moment.

In StepSecurity's replay, which ran its own rebuild because the real binaries were deleted, the upload was a JSON POST shaped like a Solana RPC request, with the archive base64-encoded inside it. StepSecurity credits that format to the advisory, which does not describe it.

The stealer used a different wallet. OVHcloud lists BHaDa for the stealer; BjVe is the wallet it ties to GlassWorm, found in the 2025 extension and the March loader. When The Hacker News wrote, nothing from the October binary had been tied to GlassWorm. OVHcloud's advisory lists five samples on VirusTotal and thanks Ivan Kwiatkowski and the VirLabs.ai malware analysis platform, ties the extension's wallet to GlassWorm, and does not name the actor behind the October uploads.

== Two patterns from earlier kill chains
layout: compare
col: Began on a personal computer | As in the 3CX / X_TRADER compromise of 2023 | Here, an extension on the developer's personal MacBook, per OVHcloud | The OVHcloud laptop had not been compromised, as far as OVHcloud knew
col: A trusted version delivered different code | As in tj-actions/changed-files in 2025, where a stolen token moved version tags | Here, assets replaced on releases published weeks or months earlier | The URLs stayed the same
Both earlier chains have their own csoh.org kill chains, linked at the end of this deck.
notes:
The page draws two comparisons with earlier kill chains on csoh.org.

The first is where the chain began. As in the 3CX / X_TRADER compromise of 2023, this chain began on an employee's personal computer: OVHcloud traces it to an extension on the developer's personal MacBook. OVHcloud says the developer's OVHcloud laptop had not been compromised, as far as it knew when it wrote the advisory.

The second is what changed for the people downloading. As in the tj-actions/changed-files compromise of 2025, where a stolen token moved existing version tags, a version people already trusted began delivering different code. Here an account with write access replaced assets on releases published weeks or months earlier, at unchanged URLs.

The page draws each comparison in one sentence and does not compare the incidents further, so keep to that.

== Six defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Rotate first, from a different machine | 06
row: Check exposure without running the binary | 03 | 04 | 06
row: Pin hashes the publisher's account cannot rewrite | 04 | 05
row: Immutable releases, covering only new ones | 04
row: Publishing accounts treated as build system | 02
row: Alert on asset changes, amended force-pushes | 02 | 04
Columns 01 and 07 stay empty. The publishing row mentions IDE extensions and the alerts row Solana RPC endpoints, both in general terms.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the seven stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own words cite that stage's facts.

Rotating first names the envchecker folder and ~/.ovh.conf: stage 06. Checking without running the binary names the 5ead8ef pseudo-version, stage 03; the 12:47 to 14:21 download window, stage 04; and the envchecker folder, stage 06. Pinning hashes cites the replaced checksums file and the customer's pinned hash, stage 04, and the install script, upgrade command and Homebrew cask, stage 05. Immutable releases answer the setting OVHcloud calls the main issue: stage 04. Treating publishing accounts as build systems cites the personal account, required 2FA and stolen keys: stage 02. The alerts cite assets replaced on old releases, stage 04, and force-pushes read by server-side timestamps, stage 02.

Columns 01 and 07 stay empty on purpose. The publishing defense says to keep publishing credentials off machines that run unvetted IDE extensions, and the alerts defense says to block Solana RPC endpoints from build hosts, but neither ties its advice to a stage, and Solana calls appear in stages 01, 02 and 07. That gap is a discussion question at the end.

== Rotate first, from another machine (stage 06)
layout: compare
col: What to rotate, and how | OVHcloud's order: rotate secrets, delete the binaries, install v0.16.0 | Rotate from another machine, per StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware | Include OVH API keys in ~/.ovh.conf | StepSecurity and The Hacker News: updating revokes nothing already copied
col: Then clean up | OVHcloud says the stealer never deletes its envchecker-* folder | That makes the folder a copy of those secrets | Per StepSecurity, delete it and rebuild any machine you cannot show is clean
None of the four sources describes a revocation trigger or destructive behavior in this stealer, so nothing argues for waiting.
notes:
This defense is for anyone who ran a swapped binary, and the page's order is rotate first.

OVHcloud's advisory lists the steps in this order: change or deactivate the secrets for every relevant service as soon as possible, delete the compromised binaries, then install v0.16.0. StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware both say to rotate from a different machine, and StepSecurity and The Hacker News point out that updating the CLI revokes nothing already copied. Include OVH API keys, which OpenSourceMalware and StepSecurity both list for rotation; the CLI keeps them in ~/.ovh.conf.

None of these four sources describes a revocation trigger or destructive behavior in this stealer, unlike the watcher in the CHAINDROP / keyv worm, so nothing here argues for waiting. OVHcloud's advisory rests on the actual binaries and describes only collection and exfiltration. The Hacker News wrote before the advisory appeared, and StepSecurity's replay ran its own rebuild.

OVHcloud says the stealer copies what it collects into the envchecker-* folder and never deletes it, so the folder is itself a copy of those secrets. StepSecurity says to delete it along with the binary, and to rebuild any machine you cannot show is clean.

== Check for exposure without running the binary
layout: compare
col: Checks that do not run it | Compare its MD5 with the advisory's appendix | Look for an envchecker-* temp folder | Only copies from 12:47 to 14:21 UTC, October 19, are affected, per OVHcloud | go version -m shows the 5ead8ef pseudo-version without running it
col: A check that runs it | OVHcloud says not to run a suspect copy; every run starts a new collection | StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware list ovhcloud version as a quick tell | On its rebuild, StepSecurity's replay exfiltrated about two seconds after it
Tied to stages 03, 04 and 06. For CI, StepSecurity says to audit jobs that fetched in that window and treat reachable credentials as exposed.
notes:
Do not run a suspect copy, even to print its version; OVHcloud's advisory says not to run it.

Instead, as OVHcloud advises, compare its MD5 against the hashes in the advisory's appendix, look for an envchecker-* folder in the temporary directory, or go by when you downloaded it: OVHcloud says only copies downloaded between 12:47 and 14:21 UTC on October 19 are affected. The go version -m command reads the build information from the file without executing it and shows the 5ead8ef pseudo-version. The Hacker News, StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware all describe that check.

StepSecurity and OpenSourceMalware also list ovhcloud version as a quick tell, but that runs the program. OVHcloud says every run starts a new collection. StepSecurity's own replay, run against its rebuild, finished exfiltrating about two seconds after that command, and The Hacker News calls running a suspect binary inadvisable.

For CI, StepSecurity says to audit any job that fetched these releases between 12:47 and 14:21 UTC on October 19, and to treat credentials reachable from those runners as exposed.

== Pinned hashes and immutable releases
layout: compare
col: Pin hashes (stages 04, 05) | On at least three releases, the checksums file was replaced with the binaries | Installers and self-update: verify a hash or attestation the release page cannot change | Consumers: pin the hash in your pipeline
col: Immutable releases (stage 04) | Per GitHub's documentation, they cover only future releases | Releases published before you enable immutability can still have assets replaced | StepSecurity recommends gh release verify-asset for v0.16.0
OVHcloud calls the missing immutability setting the main issue, and has applied it to every repository in its organization.
notes:
Two defenses answer the swap itself.

First, pin artifact hashes somewhere the publisher's account cannot rewrite. The checksums file sat in the same release as the binaries and was replaced with them, so any check against it passed. The customer's CI pinned its own copy of one hash and failed, and Homebrew's cask, kept in a separate repository, pinned the original hashes. The install script and the upgrade command checked nothing. If you ship a CLI, make the installer and the self-update path verify a hash or attestation that the release page cannot change; if you consume one, pin the hash in your pipeline.

Second, turn on immutable releases, and remember they cover only new ones. OVHcloud names the missing immutability setting as the main issue and has applied it to every repository in its organization. GitHub's documentation says immutability applies only to future releases, so a release published before you enable it can still have its assets replaced by an account with write access. An immutable release also gets an attestation that you can check with gh release verify-asset, which StepSecurity recommends for v0.16.0.

== Guard the accounts that publish (stage 02)
layout: compare
col: What the page cites | A developer's personal account, in an organization requiring 2FA | Uploads with what OVHcloud calls stolen keys | 2FA applies at sign-in; a token issued earlier is used without it
col: What to do | Limit release write access to the pipeline's identity | Keep publishing credentials off machines that run unvetted IDE extensions | OVHcloud's next steps: link release publishing to its centralized IAM
OVHcloud's next steps also include signing commits and merges, and reviewing every GitHub account attached to its organization.
notes:
The page's advice is to treat every account that can publish a release as part of your build system.

The uploads came from a developer's personal GitHub account in an organization that required 2FA, and they went through with what OVHcloud calls stolen keys. Two-factor authentication applies at sign-in, and a token issued earlier is used without it.

OVHcloud's next steps include linking any function that can publish a release to its centralized IAM, which stands for identity and access management, signing commits and merges, and reviewing every GitHub account attached to its organization. OpenSourceMalware notes v0.16.0 was published by a service account rather than a person.

The page's own advice: limit release write access to the pipeline's identity, and keep publishing credentials off machines that run unvetted IDE extensions.

== Alert on changed assets and force-pushes
layout: compare
col: Release assets (stage 04) | Alert on assets that change after publication | The releases API records each asset's uploader and timestamps | Here, a different account replaced files on releases published weeks or months earlier
col: Force-pushes (stage 02) | Alert on force-pushes to long-idle repositories, per OpenSourceMalware | Go by GitHub's server-side activity timestamps, not commit dates
OpenSourceMalware also recommends blocking Solana RPC endpoints from build hosts that have no reason to reach them.
notes:
The page's last defense is to alert on release assets that change after publication, and on amended force-pushes.

The customer confirmed the swap through the GitHub releases API, which records each asset's uploader and timestamps. It showed that a different account had replaced files on releases published weeks or months earlier.

OpenSourceMalware recommends alerting on force-pushes to long-idle repositories using GitHub's server-side activity timestamps rather than commit dates. In stage 02, it built its timeline of the account from GitHub's server-side records rather than commit dates, which the pusher controls.

It also recommends blocking Solana RPC endpoints from build hosts that have no reason to reach them. The page does not tie that advice to a particular stage.

== Markers and checks the page gives
layout: table
row: Where | What the page names
row: Build information | v0.15.1-0.20261013083505-5ead8ef9b43d+dirty, via go version -m
row: Temp directory | envchecker- plus 9 or 10 digits, never deleted, per OVHcloud
row: Network | hxxps://45.32.148[.]174/upload, which OVHcloud says can change
row: Stealer's wallet | BHaDaVbdCYFNaKZGn7yLXFneWoJHZgLiBYkHxVrjyALF
row: 2025 extension | color-picker-universal 2.8.91; 162.33.177[.]177; wallet BjVe...
row: The binaries | MD5 hashes in the advisory's appendix; five VirusTotal samples
The +dirty suffix alone is weak: The Hacker News found it on the clean v0.16.0 binary. StepSecurity names the pseudo-version and missing version flag as reliable.
notes:
These are the traces the page names, gathered in one place.

On a machine, go version -m reads the build information without running the file; the stealer build shows a pseudo-version from commit 5ead8ef, with a +dirty suffix. That suffix alone is weak, because The Hacker News found it on the clean v0.16.0 binary too. StepSecurity names the pseudo-version and the missing version flag as the reliable markers. In the temporary directory, look for envchecker- followed by nine or ten digits, which OVHcloud says is never deleted.

On the network, the upload went to 45.32.148[.]174, and OVHcloud warns the threat actor can change that URL at any moment. The stealer made Solana RPC calls through one of five public endpoints, with the wallet that begins BHaDa.

The 2025 extension was color-picker-universal 2.8.91, which contacted 162.33.177[.]177 and carried the wallet that begins BjVe.

The binaries' MD5 hashes are in the appendix of OVHcloud's advisory, which also lists five samples on VirusTotal. Compare a suspect file's hash against them rather than running it.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can the publisher's account rewrite the hashes your pipeline checks against?
check: Do your installers and self-update paths verify a hash or attestation?
check: Are your releases immutable, and what about releases published before that?
check: Which accounts can upload release assets besides the pipeline's identity?
check: Would you notice a release asset replaced weeks after publication?
check: Could you check a suspect binary for exposure without running it?
check: Do your build hosts have any reason to reach Solana RPC endpoints?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether the hashes your pipeline checks against live somewhere the publisher's account could rewrite, the way the checksums file sat in the same release as the binaries. Ask whether your installers and self-update paths verify a hash or attestation that the release page cannot change.

Ask whether your releases are immutable, and remember that immutability covers only releases published after you turn it on. Ask which accounts can upload release assets besides the pipeline's identity.

Ask whether anything would alert on a release asset replaced weeks after publication, and on force-pushes to long-idle repositories.

Ask whether your response plan checks a suspect binary by its hash, its build information or the envchecker folder, without running it. Finally, ask whether your build hosts have any reason to reach Solana RPC endpoints.

== Where does your pipeline get the hashes it checks?
layout: question
On at least three releases, the checksums file was replaced along with the binaries. A customer's CI pinned its own copy of one hash and failed.

For one download in your pipeline, who can change the hash it checks?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The page's facts: the checksums file sat in the same release as the binaries, and on at least three of the releases it was replaced after the binaries it checked, so the published hashes matched the new files. A customer whose CI pinned the SHA-256 of one archive saw the job fail and opened issue #300.

Ask each person to name one download in their own pipeline and say where its expected hash comes from, and who could change it, before you show the answer slide.

== Pin hashes where the publisher's account cannot rewrite them
layout: answer
Where the checksums file was replaced, any check against it passed. The customer's pinned copy failed the job, and Homebrew's cask, in a separate repository, pinned the original hashes.
- Consumers: pin the hash in your own pipeline.
notes:
The page's answer is to pin artifact hashes somewhere the publisher's account cannot rewrite.

The checksums file sat in the same release as the binaries and was replaced with them, so any check against it passed. The customer's CI pinned its own copy of one hash and failed. Homebrew's cask, kept in a separate repository, pinned the original hashes. The install script and the upgrade command checked nothing.

For publishers, the page says to make the installer and the self-update path verify a hash or attestation that the release page cannot change. For consumers, it says to pin the hash in your pipeline.

Bring the group's examples back to this. A hash kept beside the file, under the same account's control, was replaced along with it.

== What would you add for stages 01 and 07?
layout: question
The page ties no defense to the extension on a personal MacBook, or to the stealer's Solana calls and upload.

What would you add, and which reported detail would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gaps in the defense matrix.

The page ties its six defenses to stages 02, 03, 04, 05 and 06. It ties none to stage 01, the color-picker-universal extension on the developer's personal MacBook, or to stage 07, where the stealer made Solana RPC calls and uploaded its archive.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stages 01 and 07
layout: answer
Two defenses mention IDE extensions and Solana RPC endpoints in general terms. Any control you propose is your own; test it against reported details, like the plist-registered service or the upload address.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 01 or stage 07, and this deck adds none of its own.

Two of the page's defenses come close, and the group may argue they reach these stages. The publishing defense says to keep publishing credentials off machines that run unvetted IDE extensions. OpenSourceMalware recommends blocking Solana RPC endpoints from build hosts that have no reason to reach them. Neither ties its advice to a stage, and that argument is the group's own.

Test each proposal against the facts the page reports. For stage 01, those are the extension color-picker-universal 2.8.91 on a personal MacBook, the persistent NodeJS service registered through a plist, and its contact with 162.33.177[.]177. For stage 07, they are the Solana RPC calls through one of five public endpoints and the upload to 45.32.148[.]174, which OVHcloud says can change at any moment.

== Primary sources
layout: links
link: OVHcloud, GHSA-862f-55f5-vmvw | https://github.com/ovh/ovhcloud-cli/security/advisories/GHSA-862f-55f5-vmvw
link: ovhcloud-cli issue #300, the customer's report | https://github.com/ovh/ovhcloud-cli/issues/300
link: StepSecurity, GitHub release swap analysis | https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/ovhcloud-cli-github-release-swap-glassworm
link: OpenSourceMalware, account timeline | https://opensourcemalware.com/blog/glassworm-hacks-ovh-clouds-cli-tool
link: The Hacker News, five releases hijacked | https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/hackers-hijack-five-ovhcloud-cli.html
link: The Hacker News, GlassWorm and stolen GitHub tokens | https://thehackernews.com/2026/03/glassworm-attack-uses-stolen-github.html
link: GitHub Docs, preventing changes to your releases | https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/supply-chain-security/understanding-your-software-supply-chain/preventing-changes-to-your-releases
link: GitHub Docs, verifying the integrity of a release | https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/supply-chain-security/understanding-your-software-supply-chain/verifying-the-integrity-of-a-release
notes:
These are the primary sources the csoh.org kill chain cites. Every fact about the incident in this deck comes from that page, which draws on these.

OVHcloud's advisory is the incident record, and issue #300 is the customer's report of replaced release assets. StepSecurity analyzed the release swap and replayed the stealer on a rebuild. OpenSourceMalware built the account's timeline. The Hacker News covered the hijacked releases, and its March reporting describes GlassWorm. GitHub's two documentation pages cover preventing changes to releases and verifying a release's integrity.

The page also links the MITRE ATT&CK pages for its techniques. The background slides name their own sources, all from MITRE ATT&CK.

